Ryujinx shader translator: honour Maxwell's MOV quad-lane mask (QuadMask).

Ryujinx decodes QuadMask (MOV bits 39..42) and then ignores it, emitting an unconditional register copy.
On the hardware a MOV with QuadMask != 0xF writes only the lanes the mask selects, one bit per lane of the
2x2 pixel quad (bit0 = lane 0 .. bit3 = lane 3); 0xF is the ordinary all-lanes case and is what 127,446 of
the 127,855 MOVs in Bayonetta 3 use.

The mask matters in the Vanish effect shaders (docs/BAYONETTA3_SHADERS.md): after every TEX.3D the shader
fills registers with four MOVs whose masks are 1, 2, 4 and 8 in turn, i.e. one pass per quad lane, and each
pass must write only its own lane. Emitted unconditionally, every pass overwrites all four lanes, so the
sample of the last pass wins for the whole quad and three quarters of the data is lost.

A masked MOV becomes `dest = (lane_in_quad == ctz(mask)) ? src : dest`; the read-modify-write keeps the lanes
the mask does not select, which is what the hardware leaves in the register. Lane-in-quad is the low two bits
of the subgroup lane id, the same quantity the translator already loads for S2R LaneId and for the
quad-scoped SHFL lowering.

Applied at build time by translator/Ryujinx.Graphics.Shader.csproj to a copy in private/build; the checkout in
private/third_party stays untouched. The Ryujinx code in the context lines is MIT.
